WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.332
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.332 --> 00:00:03.790
SETH VARGO: Today
I'm going to talk

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:07.000
to you about DevOps versus SRE.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.880
This is a hot and
contentious topic.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:10.840
Which one's better?

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.220
Which one's worse?

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.340
Are they the same thing?

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.510
Are they different, competing
standards or friends?

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:20.300
For those of you that don't
know me, my name is Seth.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.340
I work on the Developer
Relations team at Google.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.730
I've been at Google about
a year and a half now.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:27.230
Prior to that, I
worked at companies

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.690
like HashiCorp
and Chef Software,

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:33.950
which are kind of regarded as
leaders in the DevOps space,

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:38.070
similar to other tools like
Puppet or Ansible or Salt.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.860
And I've been involved
in this DevOps

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:41.750
space for quite some time.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.100
I've written popular tools.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.830
I've contributed to
popular projects.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:49.970
And I've watched this movement
kind of from the beginning,

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:53.010
really since about 2007, 2008.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.830
And I'd like to share
my perspective today

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:59.060
on what I think DevOps is and
how it relates to SRE or site

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.710
reliability engineering.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.930
So with that, let's go
ahead and get started.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.970
In the beginning, there
were two groups of people.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:12.760
On the left, we have developers.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.700
Developers are
concerned with agility.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:20.080
They want to build features,
build software, and ship it

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.020
as quickly as possible to get
it in the hands of customers

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.480
and users.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.720
On the right hand side,
we have operators.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.070
Operators are concerned
with stability.

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.170
It's not broken.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:34.790
Please don't touch it.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:37.330
And this wasn't just
a personality thing.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.240
This was driven by the business.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.700
As an operator, it was
your responsibility

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.180
to make sure that the
system never went down.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.790
And when the system went down,
you got a phone call or a page

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:49.960
in the middle of the night.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:52.070
And it was your
responsibility to fix it.

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:53.930
And if you didn't do
it in a timely manner,

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:56.400
you might be fired.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.170
But then on the flip
side, the developers

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:02.400
have all of these roadmaps
and Agile and Jira tickets

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.930
that they have to complete.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.610
And if they don't complete
them and they don't get them

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.485
into production, they're
not delivering value

00:02:07.485 --> 00:02:08.910
to the business.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:10.350
And the business is suffering.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.320
And then they're at
risk of getting fired.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.620
And these are two
competing ideas.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.680
As we introduce new features
and new functionality

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:23.060
into a system, we also
introduce instability.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.660
Every new line of code we
write has the potential

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.210
to have a bug, has the
potential to have a performance

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:30.610
regression.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.010
So these are directly
competing ideas.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:34.630
We have developers
who are trying

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:38.247
to move quickly and introduce
instability and operators who

00:02:38.247 --> 00:02:40.330
are trying to slow things
down as much as possible

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.560
because it's not broken,
please don't touch it.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.830
But here's where things get
a little bit interesting.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:50.560
Developers were closer
to the business,

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.690
both physically
and metaphorically.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:58.050
Developers often sat
in the same building

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:01.980
as the directors and the
vice presidents and the CEOs.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.400
They were physically located
closer to the business decision

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.900
makers.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:12.090
Operators, on the other hand,
were often in a data center.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:16.200
Their desk might be hundreds
or thousands even miles away

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.570
from the corporate office.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:20.010
They often felt disconnected.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:24.100
They felt like their
ideas weren't being heard.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.320
Educationally, these
two groups of people

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.510
often came from
different backgrounds.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:30.820
Developers traditionally
had a software engineering

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.430
background, computer
science, information systems,

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:36.040
computer engineering, something
along those lines from a two

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.310
or four-year degree or more.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.108
Operators tended to come more
from a practical background.

00:03:42.108 --> 00:03:43.900
They might have, like,
an associates degree

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.730
or a practicum in something
like network engineering.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.370
So there's a skills
gap on both sides.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.740
Developers are really good
at algorithms and writing

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:53.700
software.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.980
Operators are really good
at understanding network

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:59.490
topologies and failure
scenarios and how redundant

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.710
does a SATA drive actually
need to be in order

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:05.220
to have this many
nines of availability?

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.370
But because developers were
closer to the business,

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.950
oftentimes they would
just write their code.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:14.460
And they would throw it over
the wall to the operators.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:16.610
That animation was nice, right?

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:19.899
Yeah, I worked
really hard on that.

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:22.392
So these developers would
throw their code over

00:04:22.392 --> 00:04:23.100
to the operators.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.410
And they'd be like,
here's my PHP.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:26.470
Please go run it for me.

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:28.470
Thanks, have a great day.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:29.970
And these operators,
remember, they

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.620
don't have a
traditional background

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:32.700
in software engineering.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.140
They may have never worked
with these languages before.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:36.887
In addition to the
responsibilities

00:04:36.887 --> 00:04:38.470
of keeping the network
up and running,

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.137
making sure hard
drives are not filling,

00:04:40.137 --> 00:04:42.420
making sure that servers
are not faulting,

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.840
they now have to understand
bugs in the application,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.150
because when that
application goes down,

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.130
they're not paging
the developer.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:51.210
They're getting paged.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.335
They're getting woken up
in the middle of the night

00:04:53.335 --> 00:04:54.923
for a bug in the
application code.

00:04:54.923 --> 00:04:56.340
But because
developers were closer

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.100
to the business, when
operators would complain,

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.680
no one would listen.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.550
So the DevOps movement
really in its purest form

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:08.780
is about breaking down that
wall between developers

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:10.070
and operators.

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:13.300
I know, that was
another nice animation.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.790
By breaking down the
barriers between developers

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:20.440
and operators and aligning
the incentives between them,

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:25.270
we can deliver software
better and faster and more

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:28.420
safely for our end users.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:30.670
What are some ways that
we can do this, though?

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:33.880
Well, one of the easiest
ways that you can break down

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.490
the barriers between
developers and operators

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:40.230
is to put them in the
same physical room.

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:42.930
This has been shown
to work successfully

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:43.890
time and time again.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:45.540
It's a time tested pattern.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:48.360
Instead of having your operators
go sit in the data center

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.610
hundreds of miles away,
put them in the same room

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:51.810
as the developers.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.490
Make them attend stand-ups.

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:56.825
Make the developers
listen to the operators.

00:05:56.825 --> 00:05:58.200
And all of a
sudden, you'll start

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.500
to have these amazing
conversations where developers

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:02.370
will try to be--

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.800
they'll try to write some
algorithm or some distributed

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.173
systems problem.

00:06:06.173 --> 00:06:07.590
And they'll have
this great thing.

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:08.970
And it works great in theory.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.270
Then you have an operator
that steps in and says,

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:12.300
so that's great.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.500
But you're telling me that
you need 40 gigs a second

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.040
for network traffic.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.360
And our data center is
running Cat5e cable,

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.000
which is maxed at, like,
10 over short distances

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.260
and five in reality.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:29.430
So that thing that you're trying
to do works great on paper

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.360
and works great on
your local laptop.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.150
But it will never
work in production

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:37.810
unless we upgrade the
cabling in our data center.

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:40.990
And just that small
collaboration saves the company

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:44.980
millions or even billions of
dollars investing in a software

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:46.990
project that cannot
be successful

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:50.942
because the underlying
hardware won't support it.

00:06:50.942 --> 00:06:52.400
And this is just
one example of how

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.210
putting developers and operators
physically in the same space

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.100
helps improve the
software delivery cycle.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:02.170
If you look at the
DevOps manifesto,

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:05.250
there's kind of
five key categories

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:07.830
that DevOps is broken into.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:10.350
The first is reducing
organizational silos.

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:12.520
And I've already talked
a little bit about this.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.660
How do we reduce the silos
that exist between developers,

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:18.300
the people writing
code, and operators,

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.990
the people making sure
that code continues to run?

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:24.240
What we quickly found,
though, is that that

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.030
was just the biggest mountain.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:29.280
After you reduce the
silos between developers

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.690
and operators, who knows what
the next giant mountain is?

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:39.590
Security, legal
review, marketing, PR.

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:41.450
All of a sudden,
those little things

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:43.820
become the new mountains.

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:46.610
And this is where the DevOps
movement in its purest

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:48.660
form-- if you're a DevOps
purist, you're like,

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:50.900
no, it's just developers
and operators.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:53.690
But in order to actually
do this successfully,

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:57.740
you'll see that it has to
involve cross-functional teams.

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.810
The same way that we
want to involve operators

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:01.520
in the development
lifecycle, we also

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:03.320
want to involve
the security team,

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:06.050
because if we involve the
security team early, instead of

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:09.470
our security and privacy review
taking six months for someone

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:11.330
who has no idea what
our product does,

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:14.475
who has no understanding
of our core business goals,

00:08:14.475 --> 00:08:16.100
we instead have
someone who's regularly

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:17.430
attending our stand-ups.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:20.270
They're regularly contributing
code and reviewing code.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:22.910
And then when it comes time
to actually do the review,

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:27.620
they may be able to complete
it in weeks instead of months,

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:31.600
ultimately delivering software
faster, but also safer,

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:34.600
because they're far less likely
to miss something, right?

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:38.380
Reviewing 100,000 lines
of code is much harder

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:41.440
to spot a bug than reviewing
1,000 lines of code

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.133
versus 100 versus one.

00:08:45.133 --> 00:08:46.800
The second piece of
the DevOps manifesto

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.030
is that we have to
accept failure as normal.

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:52.913
If you recall earlier, I talked
about fire, being fired a lot.

00:08:52.913 --> 00:08:54.580
Developers are worried
about being fired

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:55.900
if they don't ship features.

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:58.210
Operators are worried about
being fired if they don't

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:00.100
deliver 100% availability.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:01.630
This is not OK.

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:03.460
This doesn't create
a culture in which

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:06.260
people, humans, can thrive.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:08.800
We have to accept
failure as normal.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:14.820
Any system that humans build
is inherently unreliable.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:16.870
And in fact, I would
challenge anyone

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:21.220
to find a system in nature
that is 100% reliable.

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:26.000
Most systems fail
given large scale.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:29.030
So given that anecdote
or that lemma,

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:30.640
we have to accept
failure as normal.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.530
It has to be built into
the core of our business.

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:36.830
We can't just fire people every
time the system goes down.

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:39.690
Instead, we need to
plan for it in advance.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.870
So as a concrete
example, let's say

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:43.350
we're doing a
database migration.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:46.900
We're about to roll out
a database migration.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:49.430
Before we do that,
let's plan for failure.

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:52.390
Let's accept that failure
is going to happen.

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:56.320
So before we actually roll
out that database migration,

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:57.850
we're going to plan a rollback.

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:01.210
We're going to write a script
that rolls back our deployment

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:03.450
and rolls back the
changes to the data model.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:05.710
Well, why would we invest
that effort in advance?

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:06.802
It might just work.

00:10:06.802 --> 00:10:07.510
And you're right.

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:10.360
It may totally work and
that was wasted effort.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:13.810
But the problem is if it
fails, if that deployment

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:16.460
or that database
migration fails.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:18.280
Now your phones are ringing.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:19.450
Your pagers are going off.

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:21.160
Social media is blaring.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:22.330
Your boss is yelling at you.

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:23.740
Your boss's boss
is yelling at you.

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:24.490
The site is down.

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:26.050
You're losing money.

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:28.240
And you're trying
to build a plan.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:31.558
It's not the best
time to build a plan.

00:10:31.558 --> 00:10:33.100
The best time to
build a plan is when

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:36.260
there's not a lot of pressure
and you can think clearly.

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:38.750
So by accepting
failure as normal,

00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:40.987
we understand that bad
things are going to happen.

00:10:40.987 --> 00:10:42.320
There's going to be bad deploys.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:44.540
There's going to be bad
database migrations.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:46.640
How do we recover from them?

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:49.560
We need to think about
that before we deploy them.

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:55.710
On a similar note, the next
piece of the DevOps movement

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:57.855
is this idea of
implementing gradual change.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:03.150
If you work in a waterfall
software development

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:07.923
methodology, you may deploy
once a year, twice per year.

00:11:07.923 --> 00:11:09.340
And the problem
is if you're doing

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:13.330
that, you're deploying hundreds
or millions of lines of code

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:14.830
at one time.

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:17.860
And the chance that there are
no bugs in those million lines

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:20.710
of code is effectively zero.

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:23.080
It is very, very small
that there are zero bugs

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:24.373
in a million lines of code.

00:11:24.373 --> 00:11:26.540
I don't care how good of a
software engineer you are

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:28.210
or how rigorous
your discipline is.

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:30.480
There's going to be a bug.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.550
So you deploy the software.

00:11:32.550 --> 00:11:35.205
And some users
start complaining.

00:11:35.205 --> 00:11:37.580
Well, now you only have to
search through a million lines

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:39.140
of code to find the bug.

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:39.950
It's not that long.

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:43.970
It might take another
year, versus deploying

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:46.470
small incremental changes.

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:49.860
If we deploy, say, 10 or
100 lines of code at a time,

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:51.960
if all of a sudden our
monitoring starts failing,

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:54.280
our users are yelling
at us on social media,

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:56.280
our boss is telling us
that something is broken,

00:11:56.280 --> 00:12:00.060
or our internal users are
saying, hey, this is slow now,

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:02.040
we know exactly where
to look for the problem.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:04.410
The smaller that
change, the easier it

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.960
is for us to identify the
problem and the faster

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:10.110
it is for us to fix that
bug or roll back the change.

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:12.030
Rolling back a
million lines of code

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:14.930
is a lot harder than
rolling back 10.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:16.770
Now on the flip side--

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:19.680
and this is my
soapbox for a moment--

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:22.610
I don't like when people
measure DevOps success

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:23.960
in deploys per day.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:26.150
I think deploys per day
is actually a bad metric

00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:27.950
for success in this industry.

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:30.890
You'll see it a lot where people
are like, oh, we deploy 6,428

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:31.580
times per day.

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:35.360
And I'm like, great, I can
add a comma to some Java 6,486

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:36.780
times per day.

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:39.200
That's not very exciting to me.

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:42.110
But the frequency
at which you deploy

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:45.170
relative to your business
and relative to your industry

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:46.430
is a signal.

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:51.770
It does tell you how well you're
practicing these methodologies.

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:54.138
If once per week is
standard for your industry--

00:12:54.138 --> 00:12:56.180
maybe you're in something
like fintech or banking

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:58.080
where there's
regulatory requirements

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:00.198
and that's the industry
standard, that's fine.

00:13:00.198 --> 00:13:02.240
You don't have to catch
up to some startup that's

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:04.400
deploying 100 times a day.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:07.130
What's important is that you're
implementing gradual change

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:10.750
with respect to your
business and your industry.

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:13.490
End soapbox.

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:15.200
The next piece of
the DevOps movement

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:18.360
is this idea of leveraging
tooling and automation.

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:21.740
And this is where you'll
often see people confuse tools

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:23.480
like Chef, Puppet,
Ansible, Salt,

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:27.240
Terraform as DevOps tools.

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:30.447
And that's because those
tools largely guided

00:13:30.447 --> 00:13:31.280
the DevOps movement.

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:33.620
They largely supported
that DevOps movement.

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:36.410
And they coincided with
the DevOps movement.

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:38.810
So a lot of people are
like, I'm a DevOps engineer.

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:40.030
And I'm like, what do you do?

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:42.225
And they're like, I
write Terraform configs.

00:13:42.225 --> 00:13:46.130
I'm like, OK, maybe not
the appropriate job title.

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:47.510
That's fine.

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:49.880
But leverage tooling
and automation

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:53.210
is a key piece of
the DevOps movement.

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:55.898
What we found is that after
breaking down those barriers

00:13:55.898 --> 00:13:57.440
and after accepting
failure as normal

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:00.050
and implementing gradual
change, it turns out

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:03.800
there's just a lot of
work, like creating users,

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:07.190
installing packages,
building Docker containers,

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:09.920
monitoring, logging, alerting.

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:12.590
All of those things take time.

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:15.800
And if you're a company
that has 100,000 VMs

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:19.500
and you need to roll
out an open SSL patch,

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:22.410
you can't have people sit at
a keyboard, SSH into them,

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:23.970
and run yum update.

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:25.380
It doesn't scale.

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:26.815
And we quickly learned this.

00:14:26.815 --> 00:14:28.440
We quickly learned
that we have to have

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:31.200
tooling, we have to
have automation in order

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:34.320
to successfully
implement DevOps,

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:37.110
because otherwise you're just
constantly chasing your tail.

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:39.660
You're running around putting
out fires when instead we

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:41.700
need to be leveraging
automation and tooling

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:47.300
to make things repeatable and
to make a pattern out of these.

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:49.190
Another key point
is that humans are

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:52.010
inherently very bad at doing
the same thing over and over

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:53.030
and over again.

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:53.720
We get bored.

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:56.660
We get distracted--
oh, look, a butterfly--

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:59.360
whereas computers are really
good at doing the same thing

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:01.880
over and over and
over and over again.

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:04.220
So we should leverage computers
for doing the same thing

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:07.150
over and over and over again.

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:08.800
The last piece of
the DevOps movement

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:11.810
is that we have to
measure everything.

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:16.562
It doesn't matter if you
do all of these things.

00:15:16.562 --> 00:15:18.520
If at the end of the day
your boss comes to you

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:22.930
and says how much more
successful is the business

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:27.310
and you say people
are happier, that's

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:29.205
not a business justification.

00:15:29.205 --> 00:15:30.580
And I'm not saying
that we should

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:33.580
be justifying everything we do
with money or deploys per day.

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:35.200
But we have to have
numbers to support

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:37.670
the efforts that we're driving.

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:40.548
If today you don't
have any metrics

00:15:40.548 --> 00:15:42.090
and you implement
all of these DevOps

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:44.850
things and everyone
feels better and you

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:46.310
know that the
business is better,

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:48.352
but when your manager
comes to you and says, hey,

00:15:48.352 --> 00:15:51.210
we've invested $2 million
over the course of a year.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:52.390
We hired a bunch of people.

00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:53.850
We bought these
software packages.

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:55.800
We invested in this tooling.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:57.580
What do you have to show for it?

00:15:57.580 --> 00:16:02.900
And you don't have a tangible
metric to be able to point to,

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:05.780
it's unlikely that the
effort will continue.

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:08.990
And on the flip side, if you
don't measure everything,

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:12.430
how will you know if
you're actually successful?

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:16.010
You have to set clear
metrics for success.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:18.920
And that includes at the
organizational level,

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:21.950
but also at the
application level.

00:16:21.950 --> 00:16:25.300
Now, there's also a difference
between measuring everything,

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:29.200
monitoring everything, and
alerting on everything.

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:31.010
These are very different things.

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:34.690
You may measure CPU usage,
memory usage, available disc

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:36.000
space.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:39.790
You may monitor
available disc space.

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:43.910
But you may only alert if
no one can use your checkout

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:49.838
page, because if a disc
is full, that sucks.

00:16:49.838 --> 00:16:52.130
But hopefully you're running
in high availability mode.

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:54.340
And some other
service can take over.

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:58.270
And a human can clean that out
during normal business hours.

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:00.610
All too often I see
people adopting DevOps.

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:02.050
And they jump right in.

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:04.422
And they start alerting
on every metric.

00:17:04.422 --> 00:17:06.130
And they don't understand
why their phone

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:08.740
battery keeps dying.

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:10.300
CPU usage is at 30%.

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:11.440
Oh my god!

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:13.920
30%.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:15.467
Why?

00:17:15.467 --> 00:17:17.050
We need to measure
and alert on things

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:19.480
that matter to our users.

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:20.829
Is the checkout page working?

00:17:20.829 --> 00:17:23.470
Can people actually buy things
from our e-commerce site?

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:27.470
Can our internal users run their
analytics and their reports?

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:28.310
That's what matters.

00:17:28.310 --> 00:17:30.310
That's what we alert on.

00:17:30.310 --> 00:17:34.940
Then we measure the other things
so that when we get that alert,

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:36.940
we can find the root
cause or the root

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:39.140
causes that
triggered that alert.

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:45.080
So what you'll notice
here is that these are all

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:46.510
abstract ideas, though.

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:47.510
I know this is shocking.

00:17:47.510 --> 00:17:50.010
You had no idea this
slide was coming.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:51.770
These are all abstract ideas.

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.890
I gave you some examples of
how you might, say, reduce

00:17:54.890 --> 00:17:56.480
organizational silos--

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:57.795
put people in the same room.

00:17:57.795 --> 00:18:00.230
I gave you some examples
of measuring versus

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:01.790
monitoring versus alerting.

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:04.370
But they're abstract
ideas, right?

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:07.160
The way your company might
accept failure as normal

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:09.860
is very different than the
way another company might

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:12.860
accept failure as normal.

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:16.320
And what we found is
that a lot of companies

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:18.870
don't like abstract ideas.

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:21.980
They want more concrete
implementations.

00:18:21.980 --> 00:18:25.760
So story time.

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:30.170
Independently of DevOps,
around the same time,

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:32.540
though, Google started
this discipline

00:18:32.540 --> 00:18:36.350
called SRE, which stand for
Site Reliability Engineering.

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:38.240
It was an engineering-based
discipline--

00:18:38.240 --> 00:18:40.700
so kind of on the
engineering ladder,

00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:43.230
the engineering pay
scale, if you will--

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:46.640
of people who build and
maintain reliable systems.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:48.500
Their job was to
keep, at the time,

00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:52.040
things like search and ads up
and running all of the time,

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:54.660
or as much of the
time as possible.

00:18:54.660 --> 00:19:04.410
So SRE is kind of like a very
prescriptive way to do DevOps.

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:06.150
And this is why
you might hear us

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:10.200
say this phrase every so
often, which is that class

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:12.360
SRE implements DevOps.

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:14.410
Let me explain what
I mean by that.

00:19:14.410 --> 00:19:19.170
So SRE evolved
independently from DevOps.

00:19:19.170 --> 00:19:22.900
Google was really in its own
little bubble at that time.

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:25.770
And they arrived
at SRE as the way

00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:29.220
to build and maintain and run
production systems at scale.

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:31.690
And DevOps was kind of
built by the community.

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:35.300
And SRE was kind of built
by Google at the time.

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:39.260
Very recently, we've
learned that SRE should

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:40.862
be shared with the world.

00:19:40.862 --> 00:19:42.320
For quite some
time, Google thought

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:45.290
that SRE was like our
secret sauce, if you will.

00:19:45.290 --> 00:19:47.120
It was the thing that
differentiated us

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:48.165
from our competitors.

00:19:48.165 --> 00:19:49.790
It was the thing that
differentiated us

00:19:49.790 --> 00:19:51.005
from other cloud providers.

00:19:51.005 --> 00:19:52.130
We shouldn't talk about it.

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:53.030
It's a secret.

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:56.120
We didn't even post
job postings for it.

00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:59.540
But very quickly, we learned
that there's a language to SRE.

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:00.560
There's nomenclature.

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:03.020
There's ways to think
about production systems

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:05.863
that as a cloud provider,
when we go to a customer

00:20:05.863 --> 00:20:08.030
and we start saying these
words and we start talking

00:20:08.030 --> 00:20:10.070
about these concepts,
they have no idea what

00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:12.120
we're talking about.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:14.105
So we go to a customer
and we say, hey,

00:20:14.105 --> 00:20:15.980
this service has three
nines of availability.

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:17.355
And you're depending
on it, which

00:20:17.355 --> 00:20:19.430
means you can never have
more than three nines

00:20:19.430 --> 00:20:20.660
of availability.

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:25.310
And our customers just look at
us like, nines, question mark,

00:20:25.310 --> 00:20:29.580
like a German shepherd with
the head tilted sideways.

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:31.150
And this is when
we decided that SRE

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:32.620
doesn't have to be a secret.

00:20:32.620 --> 00:20:34.420
It doesn't have to
be a secret sauce.

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:38.065
And in fact, other
companies can practice SRE.

00:20:38.065 --> 00:20:39.940
And then Google stepped
outside of its bubble

00:20:39.940 --> 00:20:41.620
and realized that
there's already

00:20:41.620 --> 00:20:44.560
a practitioner-based community,
the DevOps community that

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:46.810
is trying to do this.

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:48.790
They're doing it
successfully in some cases,

00:20:48.790 --> 00:20:50.620
unsuccessfully in other cases.

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:53.030
And that's because
it's not prescriptive.

00:20:53.030 --> 00:20:57.380
DevOps is like an abstract class
or an interface in programming.

00:20:57.380 --> 00:21:01.020
It says here are the
things you should do.

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:05.160
Go figure it out, whereas SRE
is a concrete implementation

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:06.510
of that class.

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:09.150
It says here's how you
reduce organizational silos.

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:11.370
Here's how you accept
failure as normal.

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:14.650
Here is how you
measure everything.

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:18.640
And this is why we say
class SRE implements DevOps.

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:21.040
Now just like in
programming, you

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:24.520
may have an abstract
class or an implementation

00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:27.220
of an abstract class that
has other methods that

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:29.332
are not in the interface.

00:21:29.332 --> 00:21:31.540
You may have additional
methods, additional functions

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:32.748
that aren't in the interface.

00:21:32.748 --> 00:21:33.857
And SRE's the same way.

00:21:33.857 --> 00:21:35.440
There are things in
the SRE discipline

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:39.130
that aren't really part
of the DevOps interface.

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:42.030
But SRE does satisfy
the DevOps interface.

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:43.610
And let me show you how.

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:46.120
So if we go back to
the previous slide

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:49.450
where we kind of talked about
the five key areas of DevOps,

00:21:49.450 --> 00:21:52.420
how does SRE reduce
organizational silos?

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:56.440
Well, the first way
that we do this is we

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:59.200
share ownership with developers.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:03.370
So SREs at Google share
ownership with the developers

00:22:03.370 --> 00:22:07.120
by using the same
shared set of tooling

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:08.660
across the organization.

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:11.290
So there's a single set of tools
that both software engineers

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:15.070
and SREs use for
production systems.

00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:16.750
And by leveraging the
same set of tools,

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:18.620
you have software
engineers contributing.

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:20.800
You have site reliability
engineers contributing.

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:24.100
And you start to build
high-performance, very reliable

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:27.993
tooling that helps people
get their job done.

00:22:27.993 --> 00:22:29.576
On the other side
of the organization,

00:22:29.576 --> 00:22:33.120
SRE has very prescriptive
ways for which we determine

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:35.900
the availability of a system.

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:38.060
And the way that
we do that results

00:22:38.060 --> 00:22:41.990
in almost forced conversation
and forced collaboration

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:46.190
between product teams,
developers, site reliability

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:50.003
engineers, and even sales
and post-sales organizations.

00:22:53.870 --> 00:22:56.360
How does SRE accept
failure as normal?

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:59.488
Well, one of the ways in which
we encourage that collaboration

00:22:59.488 --> 00:23:01.280
I just talked about is
through these things

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:03.620
called SLOs, service
level objectives

00:23:03.620 --> 00:23:06.450
which I'll talk about
more in detail in a bit.

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:08.480
But these service level
objectives-- in addition

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:12.410
to kind of forcing collaboration
between these different groups

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:16.490
in the organization, they also
force us to admit how reliable

00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:20.510
or how unreliable
our system can be.

00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:22.850
And by having that
conversation, we immediately

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:25.730
admit that our service
is going to have faults.

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:28.160
And as a developer
and as a product owner

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:30.200
and as a site
reliability engineer,

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:32.960
I can determine how
much fault I actually

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:34.670
want my product to have.

00:23:34.670 --> 00:23:37.970
If I'm building a product that
is up against some competition,

00:23:37.970 --> 00:23:40.490
it needs to hit the
market very quickly,

00:23:40.490 --> 00:23:43.610
I may accept more risk so
that I can deploy faster,

00:23:43.610 --> 00:23:47.090
I can deploy riskier
changes, et cetera.

00:23:47.090 --> 00:23:49.370
If I'm building a product
that is targeting,

00:23:49.370 --> 00:23:52.580
say, the health care space
or the aviation space,

00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:54.620
that needs more nines
of availability.

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:56.690
That has to work
all of the time.

00:23:56.690 --> 00:23:59.117
We can't have hours
and hours of downtime.

00:23:59.117 --> 00:24:00.950
We have to have redundancy
and availability.

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:03.513
And that may slow down
our development velocity.

00:24:03.513 --> 00:24:05.930
But this is a conversation
that occurs between the product

00:24:05.930 --> 00:24:09.940
owners, the developers, and
the site reliability engineers.

00:24:09.940 --> 00:24:11.560
Additionally, as
you might expect,

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:13.360
the SRE discipline
strongly encourages

00:24:13.360 --> 00:24:15.910
blameless postmortems, which
are popular among the DevOps

00:24:15.910 --> 00:24:17.170
community as well.

00:24:17.170 --> 00:24:20.650
When an outage occurs,
it's nobody's fault.

00:24:20.650 --> 00:24:23.080
And instead, we try to find
ways in which we can improve

00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:25.000
the system moving forward.

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:27.280
Something that SRE does
that DevOps doesn't do

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:31.120
is we then generate metadata
about our postmortems

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:33.530
globally across the country--

00:24:33.530 --> 00:24:35.170
or country-- company.

00:24:35.170 --> 00:24:38.410
So for example,
at Google we know

00:24:38.410 --> 00:24:40.330
that the vast
majority of our outage

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:42.880
comes from a bad
configuration change.

00:24:42.880 --> 00:24:45.122
And that's a publicly
shared statistic.

00:24:45.122 --> 00:24:47.080
And the reason we know
that is that in addition

00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:49.870
to doing an isolated postmortem,
postmortem about an incident,

00:24:49.870 --> 00:24:53.290
we then categorize and catalog
all of that information

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:55.630
into a database where we
can run reports and gain

00:24:55.630 --> 00:24:59.200
statistics about what is
causing these overall outages.

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:01.460
And that can help you
build kind of meta-tooling

00:25:01.460 --> 00:25:03.340
and better improve
the system overall.

00:25:08.980 --> 00:25:13.790
SREs implement gradual
change by moving fast

00:25:13.790 --> 00:25:16.620
to reduce the cost of failure.

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:19.020
Again, small
iterative deployments

00:25:19.020 --> 00:25:22.410
are strongly encouraged
over large 100,000,

00:25:22.410 --> 00:25:27.180
multi 100,000 line changes.

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:31.000
Tooling and automation is really
a key piece of the SRE culture.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:34.410
In fact, SREs have this
thing called toil--

00:25:34.410 --> 00:25:37.020
T-O-I-L. I'll talk about
it more in a little bit.

00:25:37.020 --> 00:25:40.770
But toil is this
idea that you should

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:44.460
be spending on times that bring
long-term value to the system.

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:45.960
So you should be
investing your time

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:48.252
and investing your resources
in things that bring value

00:25:48.252 --> 00:25:51.150
to the system in the long run.

00:25:51.150 --> 00:25:54.650
SSHing into a system
and restarting a service

00:25:54.650 --> 00:25:56.260
doesn't bring long-term value.

00:25:56.260 --> 00:25:58.670
It brings immediate-term value.

00:25:58.670 --> 00:26:01.380
But that's something that we
should fix in the long term.

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:05.690
So SREs invest heavily
in tooling and automation

00:26:05.690 --> 00:26:08.040
in order to automate
this year's job away.

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:09.590
That's kind of an
unofficial tagline

00:26:09.590 --> 00:26:13.310
among a lot of SREs at Google
is that the manual tasks

00:26:13.310 --> 00:26:17.060
that I did this year I shouldn't
have to do again next year.

00:26:17.060 --> 00:26:18.280
I should automate those away.

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:23.900
And lastly, we
measure everything.

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:26.930
We measure systems
level metrics.

00:26:26.930 --> 00:26:31.460
But we also measure things
like toil, reliability.

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:35.270
And we'll talk more about
reliability in a bit.

00:26:35.270 --> 00:26:38.140
But hopefully this is
clear as to why we say

00:26:38.140 --> 00:26:41.630
class SRE implements DevOps.

00:26:41.630 --> 00:26:44.840
SRE is a very prescriptive,
very concrete way

00:26:44.840 --> 00:26:48.510
to satisfy the DevOps interface.

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:52.350
So given that, let's jump into
a few key areas that I think

00:26:52.350 --> 00:26:54.760
are important to discuss.

00:26:54.760 --> 00:27:01.080
The first is SLIs,
SLOs, and SLAs, oh my.

00:27:01.080 --> 00:27:05.630
An SLI is a service
level indicator,

00:27:05.630 --> 00:27:08.600
something like request
latency or requests per second

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:10.430
or failures per request.

00:27:10.430 --> 00:27:12.860
They're a point in time
or an aggregate point

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:14.960
in time of a particular
metric about a system.

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:22.440
Basically an SLI tells you
at any given moment yes or no

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:24.390
for a metric in a system.

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:26.530
Is it healthy or is it not?

00:27:26.530 --> 00:27:28.650
And you define that.

00:27:28.650 --> 00:27:31.230
So for your system,
healthy may be

00:27:31.230 --> 00:27:35.130
that the ratio of successful
requests to failed requests

00:27:35.130 --> 00:27:38.130
is less than 1%.

00:27:38.130 --> 00:27:40.630
So 99% of your requests
are successful.

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:43.950
So at any point in time, if
you imagine a point in time,

00:27:43.950 --> 00:27:46.200
that's a binary
operation, yes or no.

00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:53.100
At this moment in time, are
we up or down per that metric?

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:58.990
Then an SLO is a binding target
for a collection of those SLIs.

00:27:58.990 --> 00:28:01.500
So while an SLI is
like a point in time,

00:28:01.500 --> 00:28:03.000
if you imagine from
calculus, if you

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:06.880
were to integrate all of those
points over a time period,

00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:09.450
like a quarter or
a half or a year,

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:11.610
that's where you
get your SLO from.

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:14.710
So the SLI measures up or down.

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:16.860
And then the SLO says
how much up or down can

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:18.990
we have in a particular
time period--

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:20.370
again, like a quarter or a half.

00:28:23.072 --> 00:28:24.905
Then there's the one
that you probably heard

00:28:24.905 --> 00:28:26.800
of before, which is an SLA.

00:28:26.800 --> 00:28:30.300
An SLA is a service
level agreement.

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:31.760
This is a business
agreement that

00:28:31.760 --> 00:28:34.790
happens between a customer
or a consumer and a service

00:28:34.790 --> 00:28:36.170
provider.

00:28:36.170 --> 00:28:39.740
The SLA is typically
based on the SLO.

00:28:39.740 --> 00:28:43.430
Ideally, you want to break
your own internal SLO

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:47.150
before you break an external
SLA, because violating

00:28:47.150 --> 00:28:49.370
an SLA typically means
you have to give money

00:28:49.370 --> 00:28:52.220
or credits or
reparations in exchange

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:55.550
for violating that SLA.

00:28:55.550 --> 00:28:59.060
So SLIs drive the
SLOs, because remember

00:28:59.060 --> 00:29:02.870
the SLO is basically an
integral of the SLIs over time.

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:07.990
And then those SLOs
inform those SLAs.

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:10.120
But to give you a
better understanding

00:29:10.120 --> 00:29:12.910
of who is involved
in this process,

00:29:12.910 --> 00:29:14.980
I built this handy slide.

00:29:14.980 --> 00:29:18.340
So product, SRE, and
software engineering

00:29:18.340 --> 00:29:22.630
work together to build
those SLIs up or down.

00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:25.150
Then the SREs and the
product work directly

00:29:25.150 --> 00:29:28.600
to determine what that looks
like over a period of time.

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:32.390
And again, this is a function
of how fast do we want to move,

00:29:32.390 --> 00:29:34.750
what are we up against in
the market, how much risk

00:29:34.750 --> 00:29:38.510
are we willing to accept,
what's our target market.

00:29:38.510 --> 00:29:40.360
And then SLAs are
generally built

00:29:40.360 --> 00:29:43.210
by the sales teams
and the customers

00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:46.150
maybe with some
negotiations from product.

00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:49.960
But they have to be tighter
or less than the SLO,

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:52.960
because again, you want your
SLO to break before your SLA

00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:54.470
breaks.

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:58.880
And sometimes SLAs are also used
as part of a sales engagement

00:29:58.880 --> 00:30:01.250
where you can buy
more availability.

00:30:01.250 --> 00:30:04.460
So that's not really part
of the SRE conversation

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:08.510
other than the SLO informs
kind of the minimum baseline

00:30:08.510 --> 00:30:09.200
for the SLA.

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:15.610
So I often get this
question, which is like, OK,

00:30:15.610 --> 00:30:19.180
so you have this SLI thing
and then this SLO thing.

00:30:19.180 --> 00:30:23.886
And then what happens
when you go over?

00:30:23.886 --> 00:30:26.865
You're like, OK, system
needs to be 99.99% reliable.

00:30:26.865 --> 00:30:28.490
That gives me, like,
12 and 1/2 minutes

00:30:28.490 --> 00:30:30.230
of downtime per quarter.

00:30:30.230 --> 00:30:32.160
What happens when I'm out?

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:33.960
Like, can I just keep pushing?

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:35.230
Do I fire my developers?

00:30:35.230 --> 00:30:36.580
Like, what happens next?

00:30:36.580 --> 00:30:39.730
Well, this is where
error budgets come in.

00:30:39.730 --> 00:30:43.020
So one of the things
that's important to note

00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:46.740
is that it is nearly
impossible to find

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:49.770
a system that is 100% reliable.

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:52.230
And it is often cost
prohibitive to build

00:30:52.230 --> 00:30:55.530
a system that is 100%
reliable, especially when

00:30:55.530 --> 00:30:59.050
you're relying on
third party components.

00:30:59.050 --> 00:31:02.980
Take, for example, this
two-dimensional material phone

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:05.130
on the screen.

00:31:05.130 --> 00:31:08.570
This screen, or this
phone has 99% reliability

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:10.885
with its cellular
network carrier.

00:31:10.885 --> 00:31:13.010
This is actually pretty
standard in most countries.

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:16.720
That's your SLA with your
mobile carrier provider.

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:18.550
So your mobile
provider is saying

00:31:18.550 --> 00:31:21.100
that we will service
99% of your requests.

00:31:21.100 --> 00:31:24.120
So 1% of your
requests will fail.

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:26.390
So let's say you have
some back-end service.

00:31:26.390 --> 00:31:28.490
And you have an unlimited
amount of money.

00:31:28.490 --> 00:31:31.765
And you say, we want
100% availability.

00:31:31.765 --> 00:31:33.140
So in order to do
that, you would

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:36.950
have to run your own fiber
connections with redundancy

00:31:36.950 --> 00:31:39.860
to every cell phone tower in
the country of your choosing

00:31:39.860 --> 00:31:43.070
in order to be able to deliver
maybe 100% availability.

00:31:43.070 --> 00:31:45.320
And then you'd need your own
kind of internet backbone

00:31:45.320 --> 00:31:46.190
to run that.

00:31:46.190 --> 00:31:50.260
And you would need on-call
people pretty much constantly.

00:31:50.260 --> 00:31:53.480
Even if you did all of
that and invested millions,

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:56.140
if not billions of
dollars, the user

00:31:56.140 --> 00:32:00.250
would only experience your
app at 99% availability,

00:32:00.250 --> 00:32:03.820
because they are governed by
the least reliable components

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:05.600
of their system.

00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:08.950
So even if you have 100%
connectivity between your data

00:32:08.950 --> 00:32:12.160
centers and the cellular
network towers, that user,

00:32:12.160 --> 00:32:16.770
that end user will only
experience 99% availability,

00:32:16.770 --> 00:32:19.650
because they are governed by
the least reliable component

00:32:19.650 --> 00:32:22.460
in the system.

00:32:22.460 --> 00:32:24.790
And this is a key point
is that optimizing

00:32:24.790 --> 00:32:28.660
for 100% availability
isn't just difficult.

00:32:28.660 --> 00:32:31.120
Oftentimes it's irresponsible.

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:33.430
It is not in your
company's best interest

00:32:33.430 --> 00:32:36.070
to optimize for
100% availability.

00:32:36.070 --> 00:32:39.280
Instead, you should be
accepting failure as normal

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:41.770
and understanding
how much failure

00:32:41.770 --> 00:32:44.660
is available for your system.

00:32:44.660 --> 00:32:49.970
So like I said, that user only
experiences 99% availability.

00:32:49.970 --> 00:32:54.030
So how do you determine how much
risk your service can tolerate?

00:32:54.030 --> 00:32:55.910
How do I know how risky
my service can be?

00:32:55.910 --> 00:32:58.040
Well, there are many
factors to consider,

00:32:58.040 --> 00:33:01.370
like fault tolerance,
availability, competition

00:33:01.370 --> 00:33:03.568
in the market, how fast
you're trying to deliver,

00:33:03.568 --> 00:33:05.360
whether there's a giant
conference that you

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:07.580
need to launch at.

00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:10.400
That was a joke.

00:33:10.400 --> 00:33:16.390
But your acceptable
risk dictates your SLO.

00:33:16.390 --> 00:33:21.300
So if you have a product that
has really critical market

00:33:21.300 --> 00:33:24.090
timing and you know that
you need to deliver features

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:26.100
quickly, you may
say, hey, we're only

00:33:26.100 --> 00:33:28.950
going to offer one
nine, 90% availability,

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:30.950
because we need to be
able to move quickly.

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:33.450
And we don't want to have to
focus on reliability right now.

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:37.433
We want to focus on shipping
new product and new features.

00:33:37.433 --> 00:33:39.766
But again, if you're in a
different industry like health

00:33:39.766 --> 00:33:41.250
care or aviation
where reliability

00:33:41.250 --> 00:33:43.920
is super important, you
may say, like, hey look,

00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:46.350
we only want to focus on
reliability right now.

00:33:46.350 --> 00:33:48.780
We need to improve our
nines of availability

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:51.630
so that our customers
can trust us.

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:55.050
So as long as your
SLOs are met, you

00:33:55.050 --> 00:33:58.440
can continue pushing new
features and new product.

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:00.870
But what happens if
you violate that SLO?

00:34:00.870 --> 00:34:04.240
What happens if you've
exceeded your error budget,

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:08.070
which is the amount of failure
you can have within your SLO?

00:34:08.070 --> 00:34:10.770
Well then everyone just
plays ping pong, right?

00:34:10.770 --> 00:34:12.560
That's how this works?

00:34:12.560 --> 00:34:13.440
No.

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:14.909
You can continue deploying.

00:34:14.909 --> 00:34:19.050
Your developers can
continue building features.

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:21.780
But everything has to
focus on reliability.

00:34:21.780 --> 00:34:24.179
They can't ship new
features until we

00:34:24.179 --> 00:34:25.900
improve the reliability.

00:34:25.900 --> 00:34:27.870
So the development
efforts, the focus

00:34:27.870 --> 00:34:30.330
shifts from building
new features

00:34:30.330 --> 00:34:33.060
and delivering new features
to improving reliability

00:34:33.060 --> 00:34:35.790
and improving the
availability of the system

00:34:35.790 --> 00:34:40.570
until the budget is replenished.

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:42.403
So just like a
bank account where

00:34:42.403 --> 00:34:44.070
you get a paycheck
and the money goes in

00:34:44.070 --> 00:34:45.929
and then you buy
some Pokemon cards

00:34:45.929 --> 00:34:48.630
and then the money goes out
and then you get more money,

00:34:48.630 --> 00:34:50.880
the error budget
works the same way.

00:34:50.880 --> 00:34:54.540
It's that after we've exhausted
our bank account of error,

00:34:54.540 --> 00:34:56.940
we can only focus
on reliability.

00:34:56.940 --> 00:35:00.060
We can only deploy features that
focus on reliability or bugs

00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:03.020
that improve reliability.

00:35:03.020 --> 00:35:05.350
So what does that
actually look like?

00:35:05.350 --> 00:35:08.290
Well, here's kind of
a pretty dumb version

00:35:08.290 --> 00:35:09.460
of an internal graph.

00:35:09.460 --> 00:35:11.680
So on the top, we
have the health

00:35:11.680 --> 00:35:13.750
of the system,
which is measuring

00:35:13.750 --> 00:35:16.690
the number of requests that
are under 300 milliseconds

00:35:16.690 --> 00:35:18.790
at any point in time.

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:21.070
So this is like an SLI.

00:35:21.070 --> 00:35:23.830
In the middle in the green,
we have the compliance,

00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:26.200
the up or down.

00:35:26.200 --> 00:35:28.220
Are the sum of the
requests under 300 seconds?

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:29.830
So you'll notice that that's
more of a step function,

00:35:29.830 --> 00:35:31.010
because it's binary.

00:35:31.010 --> 00:35:31.780
It's yes or no.

00:35:31.780 --> 00:35:34.450
You're either above
or below the line.

00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:35.950
And then at the
very bottom, we have

00:35:35.950 --> 00:35:39.170
the budget of non-compliant
requests in our SLO budget.

00:35:39.170 --> 00:35:40.960
So how many requests
are remaining?

00:35:40.960 --> 00:35:43.330
What you'll notice there is
pretty far in the right hand

00:35:43.330 --> 00:35:46.900
side, there's a drastic
increase in our latency.

00:35:46.900 --> 00:35:48.160
So you'll notice the--

00:35:48.160 --> 00:35:51.050
it's a little bit confusing,
because the chart goes down,

00:35:51.050 --> 00:35:53.020
which is a decrease in
the number of requests

00:35:53.020 --> 00:35:56.230
under 300 milliseconds,
which is an increase

00:35:56.230 --> 00:35:59.350
in the number of requests
over 300 milliseconds.

00:35:59.350 --> 00:36:02.260
So we see a drastic
increase in latency

00:36:02.260 --> 00:36:03.760
that obviously
causes our compliance

00:36:03.760 --> 00:36:05.970
to drop significantly.

00:36:05.970 --> 00:36:08.120
And then you can
start to see that just

00:36:08.120 --> 00:36:10.820
like my bank
account, all of that

00:36:10.820 --> 00:36:13.610
starts to drop in our budget.

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:17.840
We start to see a massive drop,
because we have a prolonged

00:36:17.840 --> 00:36:20.560
violation of our SLO.

00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:25.563
And it's a steady decline
until we're back in compliance.

00:36:25.563 --> 00:36:27.980
And then we gradually gain a
little bit more error budget,

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:30.313
because time is moving on.

00:36:30.313 --> 00:36:31.730
And then we start
declining again,

00:36:31.730 --> 00:36:35.090
because we have a regression.

00:36:35.090 --> 00:36:37.880
And these types of charts help
inform how much availability

00:36:37.880 --> 00:36:39.670
your system actually has.

00:36:39.670 --> 00:36:42.360
And when we're at the
bottom of that budget,

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:45.310
we have to focus on reliability.

00:36:45.310 --> 00:36:49.240
So you might ask yourself, well,
what just prevents developers

00:36:49.240 --> 00:36:52.380
from saying, well, like,
I know I'm at the budget.

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:54.890
But this is an
important feature.

00:36:54.890 --> 00:36:56.410
Why can't I deploy it?

00:36:56.410 --> 00:37:00.230
Well, they can.

00:37:00.230 --> 00:37:03.090
But they might lose
their SRE support.

00:37:03.090 --> 00:37:05.100
So remember that
SRE is a discipline.

00:37:05.100 --> 00:37:08.300
It's a separate organization
that partners with the software

00:37:08.300 --> 00:37:09.890
and product teams.

00:37:09.890 --> 00:37:11.840
And if the product team
and the software team

00:37:11.840 --> 00:37:15.440
are not willing to be adequate
partners, the SREs will gladly

00:37:15.440 --> 00:37:19.110
hand you the pager and
walk away, at which point

00:37:19.110 --> 00:37:21.870
now developers are not only
responsible for building

00:37:21.870 --> 00:37:24.720
new features, they're also
responsible for the reliability

00:37:24.720 --> 00:37:26.345
of the system.

00:37:26.345 --> 00:37:27.720
And I guarantee
you they'll start

00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:31.055
improving the reliability of the
system that night at 2:00 AM.

00:37:35.042 --> 00:37:36.500
Another thing I
want to talk about,

00:37:36.500 --> 00:37:39.320
because I get a lot of
questions about it, is toil.

00:37:39.320 --> 00:37:40.700
It's a fun word, toil.

00:37:40.700 --> 00:37:46.090
It's like foil,
but with a T. Toil

00:37:46.090 --> 00:37:48.160
is best described
as what it's not.

00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:50.470
It's one of those weird things.

00:37:50.470 --> 00:37:51.960
Toil is not email.

00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:53.140
It's not expense reports.

00:37:53.140 --> 00:37:54.070
It's not meetings.

00:37:54.070 --> 00:37:55.300
It's not traveling.

00:37:55.300 --> 00:37:57.337
These are all things we
call overhead, things

00:37:57.337 --> 00:37:59.170
that are required to
do your job that pretty

00:37:59.170 --> 00:38:00.545
much everyone in
the organization

00:38:00.545 --> 00:38:02.740
needs to accomplish.

00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:07.210
Toil is actually something
that is manual, repetitive.

00:38:07.210 --> 00:38:10.450
Most importantly, it's
devoid of long-term value.

00:38:10.450 --> 00:38:15.080
It's often incredibly tactical
and highly automatable.

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:18.170
Classic example of toil is
like SSHing into a system

00:38:18.170 --> 00:38:20.510
and restarting a service
because it's out of memory

00:38:20.510 --> 00:38:25.260
or it's spiking CPU
usage or something crazy.

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:27.230
That's incredibly tactical.

00:38:27.230 --> 00:38:29.360
Right now, doing that,
graphs go back down.

00:38:29.360 --> 00:38:30.740
Everyone's happy.

00:38:30.740 --> 00:38:32.840
But it is devoid
of long-term value,

00:38:32.840 --> 00:38:36.063
because that service is likely
going to out of memory again.

00:38:36.063 --> 00:38:37.730
And you're going to
have to do it again.

00:38:37.730 --> 00:38:39.650
And it's going to be repetitive.

00:38:39.650 --> 00:38:41.180
And it's manual.

00:38:41.180 --> 00:38:43.580
But it is very easy to automate.

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:46.550
That's a classic example of
something that's very toily.

00:38:46.550 --> 00:38:49.190
And in the SRE discipline,
we measure toil

00:38:49.190 --> 00:38:51.590
and we talk about
toil because it's

00:38:51.590 --> 00:38:53.610
a very negative
consequence of the job.

00:38:56.350 --> 00:38:59.740
If you're constantly
working with toil,

00:38:59.740 --> 00:39:01.870
this can lead to
career stagnation.

00:39:01.870 --> 00:39:05.732
No one got promoted
for restarting servers.

00:39:05.732 --> 00:39:07.440
How many people got
promoted because they

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:09.020
restarted a server once?

00:39:09.020 --> 00:39:09.960
Right, exactly.

00:39:09.960 --> 00:39:11.910
No one.

00:39:11.910 --> 00:39:17.910
And at the same time, a little
bit of toil is also good.

00:39:17.910 --> 00:39:19.625
So there's a careful
balance here.

00:39:19.625 --> 00:39:22.250
And every year, we kind of send
out a survey to all of the SREs

00:39:22.250 --> 00:39:22.770
at Google.

00:39:22.770 --> 00:39:26.850
And most SREs aim somewhere
between 10% to 20%

00:39:26.850 --> 00:39:28.740
of toil in their job.

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:31.127
So you might ask yourself,
where's toil good?

00:39:31.127 --> 00:39:32.460
It sounds like this is terrible.

00:39:32.460 --> 00:39:33.700
I should automate everything.

00:39:33.700 --> 00:39:36.570
Well, if once per
year you have to do

00:39:36.570 --> 00:39:39.390
some very complex operation,
like some aggregate report

00:39:39.390 --> 00:39:42.450
that spans multiple systems
and it's very complex

00:39:42.450 --> 00:39:45.120
and it would take you
20 hours to automate it,

00:39:45.120 --> 00:39:47.460
but it just takes you 15
minutes to do it manually

00:39:47.460 --> 00:39:49.860
and you only need to
do it once per year,

00:39:49.860 --> 00:39:52.080
it is not a good
return on investment

00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:54.270
for you to spend
time automating that.

00:39:54.270 --> 00:39:56.728
Instead, you should make sure
that other people on the team

00:39:56.728 --> 00:39:59.350
know how to do it so that you're
not one person, documented,

00:39:59.350 --> 00:40:00.450
et cetera.

00:40:00.450 --> 00:40:02.190
But you shouldn't
waste more time

00:40:02.190 --> 00:40:05.490
automating something than
it takes to do it over time.

00:40:05.490 --> 00:40:08.580
It would take a hundred years
for you to reclaim that time.

00:40:08.580 --> 00:40:11.110
No, it would take you 125
years to reclaim that time.

00:40:11.110 --> 00:40:13.110
It's just not worth it.

00:40:13.110 --> 00:40:16.020
Toil is also an excellent
way for newcomers,

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:19.238
like interns or people new to
the team, to learn the system.

00:40:19.238 --> 00:40:21.030
There's no-- I mean,
there are better ways.

00:40:21.030 --> 00:40:23.250
But one of the best ways to
learn a production system

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:24.900
is to explore it.

00:40:24.900 --> 00:40:27.000
And being able to poke
around and understand

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:29.292
systems and understand how
they work with one another--

00:40:29.292 --> 00:40:30.080
that's very toily.

00:40:30.080 --> 00:40:31.830
But it's also a great
learning opportunity

00:40:31.830 --> 00:40:34.910
for newcomers to the team.

00:40:34.910 --> 00:40:36.770
Another value for toil is that--

00:40:36.770 --> 00:40:39.760
how many people have
ever had a bad day?

00:40:39.760 --> 00:40:42.020
Wow, that's like-- you are
all so positive people.

00:40:44.710 --> 00:40:48.027
How many people have ever had a
straight six-hour meeting day?

00:40:48.027 --> 00:40:49.360
I know that you've all had that.

00:40:49.360 --> 00:40:50.170
Yeah, right?

00:40:50.170 --> 00:40:51.580
And then at the
end of six hours,

00:40:51.580 --> 00:40:53.988
you have 475 unread emails.

00:40:53.988 --> 00:40:55.780
You basically feel like
you've accomplished

00:40:55.780 --> 00:40:57.430
nothing for the day.

00:40:57.430 --> 00:40:59.950
And then you get a page
that this server's out

00:40:59.950 --> 00:41:02.130
of disk space.

00:41:02.130 --> 00:41:03.395
And you can fix it.

00:41:03.395 --> 00:41:04.770
Then you can go
home for the day.

00:41:04.770 --> 00:41:08.170
And you feel like you've done
something with your life.

00:41:08.170 --> 00:41:10.740
Toil can actually satisfy that.

00:41:10.740 --> 00:41:14.130
Toil provides that
instant gratification,

00:41:14.130 --> 00:41:16.890
but in small doses.

00:41:16.890 --> 00:41:20.310
At a large, grand scale, we want
to eliminate toil as much as

00:41:20.310 --> 00:41:21.520
possible.

00:41:21.520 --> 00:41:23.207
We want SREs to be
focused on improving

00:41:23.207 --> 00:41:25.290
the reliability of the
system and the availability

00:41:25.290 --> 00:41:28.080
of the system, not
performing toily tasks

00:41:28.080 --> 00:41:31.020
unless absolutely necessary.

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:33.680
So I've given you kind
of a brief overview.

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:36.380
You may ask yourself,
where can I learn more?

00:41:36.380 --> 00:41:40.340
There's this happy link at
Google.com/SRE where you can

00:41:40.340 --> 00:41:43.830
find all of this information
and a bunch more,

00:41:43.830 --> 00:41:47.600
including two free books--

00:41:47.600 --> 00:41:51.240
that's free e-books.

00:41:51.240 --> 00:41:53.880
The first is the "Site
Reliability Engineering" book.

00:41:53.880 --> 00:41:56.910
I like to call this the
theoretical calculus book.

00:41:56.910 --> 00:41:59.200
And then there's the "Site
Reliability Workbook,"

00:41:59.200 --> 00:42:03.030
which is like the
problem set, if you will.

00:42:03.030 --> 00:42:05.040
They're both excellent books.

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:06.660
The SRE book talks
a little bit more

00:42:06.660 --> 00:42:08.817
about the theory
and the history.

00:42:08.817 --> 00:42:10.650
The workbook is a little
bit more practical,

00:42:10.650 --> 00:42:11.775
a little bit more hands on.

00:42:11.775 --> 00:42:14.550
And there's an entire section
about how DevOps and SRE relate

00:42:14.550 --> 00:42:15.870
to one another.

00:42:15.870 --> 00:42:17.550
Both of these are free.

00:42:17.550 --> 00:42:19.540
Did I mention that they're free?

00:42:19.540 --> 00:42:22.740
And you can download
them for free.

00:42:22.740 --> 00:42:24.840
If you would like
a print version,

00:42:24.840 --> 00:42:29.320
you can purchase them from
your favorite book vendor.

00:42:29.320 --> 00:42:35.010
So to kind of conclude
here, is SRE DevOps 2.0?

00:42:35.010 --> 00:42:37.080
No, it's not trying to be.

00:42:37.080 --> 00:42:41.630
I also hate when people
put 2.0 on things.

00:42:41.630 --> 00:42:44.050
Is SRE trying to
overtake DevOps?

00:42:44.050 --> 00:42:46.443
No, lol.

00:42:46.443 --> 00:42:48.610
And I learned yesterday
that I'm the only person who

00:42:48.610 --> 00:42:49.555
pronounces lol.

00:42:49.555 --> 00:42:54.980
I thought people
pronounced it, lol.

00:42:54.980 --> 00:42:57.470
Can I adopt both DevOps and SRE?

00:42:57.470 --> 00:42:58.670
Yes.

00:42:58.670 --> 00:43:01.220
As I said before, if
you implement DevOps,

00:43:01.220 --> 00:43:04.550
if you follow the SRE
workbook and SRE book,

00:43:04.550 --> 00:43:07.730
you will be satisfying
the DevOps interface.

00:43:07.730 --> 00:43:10.130
On the flip side, if you
satisfy the DevOps interface,

00:43:10.130 --> 00:43:11.973
you might not be doing SRE.

00:43:11.973 --> 00:43:13.640
There are a number
of other disciplines,

00:43:13.640 --> 00:43:16.100
like VictorOps has a
thing called ProdOps.

00:43:16.100 --> 00:43:18.890
There are other disciplines that
satisfy the DevOps interface,

00:43:18.890 --> 00:43:20.160
but are not SRE.

00:43:22.840 --> 00:43:23.990
Is DevOps dead?

00:43:23.990 --> 00:43:25.190
I get this question a lot.

00:43:25.190 --> 00:43:27.930
No one really talks
about DevOps anymore.

00:43:27.930 --> 00:43:30.320
I think that's
because it's not dead.

00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:32.270
It's just assumed now.

00:43:32.270 --> 00:43:34.850
It's assumed that
you're following some

00:43:34.850 --> 00:43:38.010
or all of these principles.

00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:39.020
Is my talk over?

00:43:39.020 --> 00:43:41.770
Yes.

00:43:41.770 --> 00:43:43.730
We have about five
minutes for questions.

00:43:43.730 --> 00:43:46.750
There are two microphones
in the middle of the room.

00:43:46.750 --> 00:43:48.910
If anyone has questions,
please line up

00:43:48.910 --> 00:43:52.675
at the microphone
for the recording.

00:43:52.675 --> 00:43:54.050
While folks are
lining up, if you

00:43:54.050 --> 00:43:56.008
have a question that you
don't feel comfortable

00:43:56.008 --> 00:43:59.120
asking on the microphone
or on the recording, that

00:43:59.120 --> 00:44:00.590
is my Twitter handle.

00:44:00.590 --> 00:44:02.870
Feel free to tweet
at me at any time.

00:44:02.870 --> 00:44:05.000
My direct messages
are also open.

00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:06.950
So if you have a
question that you

00:44:06.950 --> 00:44:09.740
want to ask that you don't feel
comfortable asking in public,

00:44:09.740 --> 00:44:11.330
feel free to send
me a direct message.

00:44:11.330 --> 00:44:13.143
I will get back to you
as soon as possible.

00:44:13.143 --> 00:44:14.060
Thank you all so much.

00:44:14.060 --> 00:44:15.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:44:15.260 --> 00:44:18.910
[APPLAUSE]

